WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.500
Why does a cut-off axon fall apart?

2
00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:08.100
Is it starving, or following orders?

3
00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:14.520
In 1850, Augustus Waller saw cut frog nerve fibres break up beyond the cut.

4
00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:17.140
He read it as a matter of nutrition.

5
00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:21.670
If it is starving, only replacing what it lacks should save it.

6
00:00:22.370 --> 00:00:25.210
If it is a program, deleting one part should stop it.

7
00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:27.580
What mouse would decide it?

8
00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:37.533
One missing a part of the program, whose cut axons stay whole.

9
00:00:37.533 --> 00:00:39.933
But the first clue came by accident.

10
00:00:40.967 --> 00:00:44.067
In the slow mouse, cut axons last two weeks or more.

11
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.700
Its mutation makes an odd fusion with an enzyme that makes NAD+.

12
00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:51.480
Remember that enzyme.

13
00:00:52.483 --> 00:00:54.443
So it might replace what the stump lacks.

14
00:00:55.323 --> 00:00:57.003
That alone cannot prove a program.

15
00:00:57.983 --> 00:00:59.563
Then came the deciding mouse.

16
00:01:00.523 --> 00:01:05.623
Mice lacking an ordinary gene, Sarm1, kept cut axons intact for a long time.

17
00:01:06.443 --> 00:01:07.743
The stump carries a program.

18
00:01:11.767 --> 00:01:13.887
Why is a long axon so exposed?

19
00:01:15.107 --> 00:01:20.327
Picture a metre-long axon, 10 micrometres wide, holding about a thousand times the

20
00:01:20.327 --> 00:01:23.047
volume of a cell body 50 micrometres wide.

21
00:01:23.867 --> 00:01:25.747
It depends on deliveries from that body.

22
00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:31.460
The key delivery is NMNAT2, the least stable enzyme of its family.

23
00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:36.860
It turns NMN, nicotinamide mononucleotide, into NAD+.

24
00:01:38.483 --> 00:01:42.643
The slow mouse's fusion protein is a longer-lived NMNAT.

25
00:01:43.403 --> 00:01:45.323
It keeps that job going after a cut.

26
00:01:46.817 --> 00:01:48.697
After a cut, it stops arriving.

27
00:01:49.597 --> 00:01:52.097
What happens to NMN, and to NAD+?

28
00:01:58.517 --> 00:02:01.517
NMN piles up, and NAD+ plus falls.

29
00:02:02.337 --> 00:02:04.417
Their ratio climbs as the enzyme fails.

30
00:02:05.867 --> 00:02:07.667
SARM1 senses that ratio.

31
00:02:08.547 --> 00:02:11.647
It is a ring of eight, held off while NAD+ Plus is bound.

32
00:02:12.667 --> 00:02:15.767
In the leading model, NMN competes for that site.

33
00:02:16.627 --> 00:02:20.367
Its binding shifts the outer domains and frees the TIR domains.

34
00:02:21.517 --> 00:02:26.557
The freed TIR domains come together into an enzyme that cleaves NAD+.

35
00:02:27.247 --> 00:02:31.957
In this model, each loss may push the ratio higher, and the slow drift becomes a

36
00:02:31.957 --> 00:02:32.957
crash.

37
00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:41.640
Energy fails, calcium rises, and calpains, proteases switched on by calcium, cut the

38
00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:42.640
cytoskeleton.

39
00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:50.520
Mice lacking NMNAT2 from the start die around birth.

40
00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:52.580
Their growing axons stall.

41
00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:55.900
Remove Sarm1 too. What happens?

42
00:03:02.433 --> 00:03:04.853
Their axons grow, and the mice survive.

43
00:03:05.813 --> 00:03:08.873
Losing NMNAT2 works by tripping SARM1.

44
00:03:11.267 --> 00:03:12.887
And it is not apoptosis.

45
00:03:13.727 --> 00:03:16.407
The stump degenerates even when caspases are blocked.

46
00:03:18.617 --> 00:03:20.937
So the cut-off piece is not simply starving.

47
00:03:21.877 --> 00:03:24.537
It carries a switch that waits for the deliveries to stop.
